    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d857">

                <pb facs="0929" n="919" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg919"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.0031/3–2149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-21T12:00:00+08:00">March 21,
                            1949—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 21—12:51 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>948. British Ambassador<anchor n="8" xml:id="d857-f1"/> informs me British cannot
                    use noncertified consular invoices (reContel 5 [to] Nanking, 922 March 17, 7 p.
                    m., to Department,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d857-f2"/> 44 Hong Kong, 86 Peiping, 26
                    Tientsin) as means for putting pressure on Communists to deal with western
                    consulates since British don’t issue consular invoices. British are further
                    studying question.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 553, repeated Hong Kong 46, Department and Tientsin 29.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
